<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ohio River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> River is a major river of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, flowing about 1,580 km from Pittsburgh to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. In the 18th century the Ohio country was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Native nations, and after 1783 the river was the line that the Western Confederacy demanded as the limit of American settlement. It later divided free and slave states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ohio-river.jpg" alt="Ohio River" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ohiorivermap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Portsmouth Earthworks, at the meeting of the Scioto and Ohio rivers, combined circular enclosures, two large horseshoe-shaped enclosures and three sets of parallel-walled roads, one of which crossed the <b>Ohio River</b> into Kentucky.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Indiana, lying between Lake Michigan to the northwest and the <b>Ohio River</b> to the south, was admitted to the Union as the 19th state of the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Confederation Congress set out how lands north-west of the <b>Ohio River</b> would be governed and admitted as new states.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Paris of 1783 gave the United States land north of the <b>Ohio River</b> that its Native inhabitants had never ceded.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
